# Cloudflare Pages reads this file from the root of the deployed output. Astro copies
# `public/` verbatim, so this lands at `dist/_headers` and needs no build step.
#
# THIS FILE IS THE ONLY THING THAT CACHES THE SITE'S ASSETS. It used to share the job
# with a `[[headers]]` block in `netlify.toml`; that file and the Netlify project are both
# deleted, so there is no second copy of this rule and no host default behind it. Measured
# on production the day the toml went, one host, one deploy, two answers: `/` returns
# `public, max-age=0, must-revalidate` and `/_astro/icons.*.css` returns the rule below.
# The only difference between them is that one path is named here.
#
# Every filename under `/_astro/` carries a hash of its own contents, so a URL there can
# never mean two things, and a year of `immutable` costs nothing. Without this rule each
# hashed asset spends a render-blocking round trip being told it has not changed —
# measured at 168ms and 175ms, transferSize 300, a 304 carrying no content.
#
# THE HASHING IS THE PRECONDITION, not a detail. `tests/build-output.test.ts` asserts
# it separately, because the day one asset lands here without a hash this rule pins a
# stale file for a year and nothing else would say so.
#
# Cloudflare lower-cases header names on the way out, so the case written here does not
# reach a reader either way.

/_astro/*
  cache-control: public, max-age=31536000, immutable
